WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.440
In the very first episode of NCIS, titled
"Yankee White," the death of a Marine on Air

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.230
Force One triggers a mess of jurisdictional
complications.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:14.230
The question this episode raises is who claims
jurisdiction over the death of a Naval commander

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.730
who dies on Air Force One?

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.200
Do Gibbs and his team have any right to fly
away with the president's plane in order to

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.570
hold such jurisdiction?

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.220
First, let's break the events of the episode
down: The second the president walks off the

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.110
plane, the Secret Service loses jurisdiction,
and it gets handed off to the FBI, hence FBI

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.640
Agent Fornell's presence at the crime scene.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.960
The Secretary of the Navy instructs that,
since NCIS has a horse in the race in the

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.530
form of a dead officer, they would have jurisdiction
to take custody of the body in the real world.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:48.780
However, does that really give them the right
to snatch the president's plane to ensure

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.210
they keep custody?

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:54.060
And if they can't take the plane, how is it
that we never hear about anyone getting in

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.780
trouble for that stunt thereafter?

